Below is a quick‑start guide you can copy‑paste into any project.  
It shows **how to pick a greeting automatically from the user’s geographic location** (using the IP address) and then fall back to the browser‑language greeting if the IP‑lookup fails or you prefer a client‑side solution.

---

## 1️⃣  The core idea

| Step | What you do | Why it works |
|------|-------------|--------------|
| **A. Detect the client’s IP** | In a server‑side endpoint (Node, Python, Go, etc.) read `req.ip` (or the `X‑Forwarded‑For` header when behind a proxy). | The IP is the only reliable piece of data you have before the page even loads. |
| **B. Call the “greeting‑by‑IP” API** | `getGreetingByIP({ ip: clientIp })` | The service maps the IP → country/region → appropriate language greeting. |
| **C. (Optional) Fallback to browser language** | If the IP‑lookup returns an error or you want a client‑side check, call `getGreeting({ language: navigator.language })`. | The browser already knows the user’s preferred language; this is a good safety net. |
| **D. Return the greeting to the front‑end** | Send the text (or JSON) back to the UI and display it. | The user sees a culturally‑appropriate “Hello”. |

---

## 2️⃣  Example implementations

### Node.js / Express (server‑side)

```js
import express from "express";
import fetch from "node-fetch";   // or any HTTP client you like

const app = express();
const PORT = 3000;

// Helper: call the API that you described
async function getGreetingByIP(ip) {
  const resp = await fetch("https://api.example.com/getGreetingByIP", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ ip })
  });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error("API error");
  return await resp.json();   // { greeting: "¡Hola!" }  (or plain text)
}

// Optional fallback: browser language
async function getGreetingByLang(lang) {
  const resp = await fetch("https://api.example.com/getGreeting", {
    method: "POST",
    headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ language: lang })
  });
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error("API error");
  return await resp.json();
}

// -------------------------------------------------
app.get("/", async (req, res) => {
  // 1️⃣ Grab the client IP (works behind most proxies)
  const clientIp =
    req.headers["x-forwarded-for"]?.split(",")[0]?.trim() ||
    req.socket?.remoteAddress;

  try {
    // 2️⃣ Try the IP‑based greeting first
    const { greeting } = await getGreetingByIP(clientIp);
    res.send(`<h1>${greeting}</h1>`);
  } catch (e) {
    // 3️⃣ Fallback to browser language (sent as a query param by the front‑end)
    const lang = req.query.lang || "en";
    const { greeting } = await getGreetingByLang(lang);
    res.send(`<h1>${greeting}</h1>`);
  }
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`🚀 listening on ${PORT}`));
```

**What you need to change**

* Replace `https://api.example.com/...` with the real endpoint that implements the `getGreetingByIP` and `getGreeting` functions you described.
* If the API returns plain text instead of JSON, just read `await resp.text()`.

---

### Front‑end (plain JavaScript) – client‑side fallback

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="UTF-8"><title>Greeting Demo</title></head>
<body>
  <h1 id="greeting">…loading…</h1>

  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Ask the server for a greeting based on IP (you already have an endpoint)
    fetch("/?lang=" + encodeURIComponent(navigator.language))
      .then(r => r.text())
      .then(html => {
        document.getElementById("greeting").innerHTML = html;
      })
      .catch(err => {
        console.error("Failed to get greeting:", err);
        // As a last resort, just show English
        document.getElementById("greeting").textContent = "Hello!";
      });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

*The client sends its `navigator.language` as a query‑string fallback; the server can ignore it if the IP‑lookup succeeded.*

---

### Python (FastAPI) – same logic, different stack

```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, Request
import httpx

app = FastAPI()
API_BASE = "https://api.example.com"

async def greeting_by_ip(ip: str) -> str:
    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:
        r = await client.post(f"{API_BASE}/getGreetingByIP", json={"ip": ip})
        r.raise_for_status()
        data = r.json()
        return data["greeting"]   # adjust if the shape differs

async def greeting_by_lang(lang: str) -> str:
    async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:
        r = await client.post(f"{API_BASE}/getGreeting", json={"language": lang})
        r.raise_for_status()
        return r.json()["greeting"]

@app.get("/")
async def root(request: Request, lang: str = "en"):
    # 1️⃣ Extract client IP (FastAPI gives you the raw socket address)
    client_ip = request.client.host

    try:
        greeting = await greeting_by_ip(client_ip)
    except Exception:
        # 2️⃣ Fallback to language
        greeting = await greeting_by_lang(lang)

    return f"<h1>{greeting}</h1>"
```

---

## 3️⃣  Why “IP → location → greeting” works well

| Situation | IP‑based greeting shines | Browser‑language fallback shines |
|-----------|--------------------------|---------------------------------|
| **User behind VPN or corporate proxy** | May map to the VPN’s data‑center location – still a *reasonable* default (e.g., “Hello” in English). | Browser language is still accurate for the user’s personal preference. |
| **User’s device language is set to a non‑native language** (e.g., a French speaker using an English OS) | IP gives you the *real* country, so you can show “Bonjour”. | The fallback respects the user’s explicit language choice, which they may have set on purpose. |
| **Stateless API (no cookies, no auth)** | You can greet the user on the very first request, before any session is created. | Works even when the client blocks `Accept‑Language` headers. |

---

## 4️⃣  Handy extra utilities

* **List all supported languages** – call `getSupportedLanguages()` once at startup and cache the result.  
  ```js
  const SUPPORTED = await fetch("/api/getSupportedLanguages").then(r=>r.json());
  console.log("We can greet in:", SUPPORTED);
  ```

* **Custom format** – if you need JSON for a SPA, use `getGreetingWithFormat({ format: "json", language: "es" })`.  
  The response will be `{ "greeting": "¡Hola!" }`.

* **Rate‑limit protection** – most IP‑to‑greeting services are cheap, but you can add a simple in‑memory cache keyed by IP (e.g., `Map<string, {greeting:string, ttl:number}>`) to avoid hitting the API on every request.

---

