Below is a practical, step‑by‑step guide you can follow to add **SoundCloud music playback** to any website (plain HTML, a modern JavaScript framework, or a CMS).  
It covers the three most common ways developers integrate SoundCloud:

| Integration method | When to use it | What you get |
|--------------------|----------------|--------------|
| **Embedded widget** (iframe) | Quick “drop‑in” playback, no custom UI needed | Fully‑featured player (play/pause, share, download, etc.) that you can style a little with CSS |
| **SoundCloud Widget JavaScript API** | You want a custom UI (buttons, track list, progress bar) but still rely on the hosted player | Full control over playback (play, pause, seek, volume, events) while still using SoundCloud’s streaming infrastructure |
| **SoundCloud REST API + OAuth** | You need deep integration: user‑specific playlists, likes, uploading tracks, analytics, or you want to stream audio yourself (e.g., via the HTML5 `<audio>` element) | Access to all public & private data, ability to create tracks, manage playlists, and (with the proper scope) stream tracks directly |

Below you’ll find the **complete workflow** for each method, plus code snippets you can copy‑paste.

---

## 1️⃣ Quick‑Start: Embed a Track or Playlist with an iframe

The simplest way to get a player on your page is to use SoundCloud’s embed code.

### Step‑by‑step

1. **Find the track/playlist URL** you want to embed, e.g. `https://soundcloud.com/artistname/track-name`.
2. **Generate the embed code** using SoundCloud’s oEmbed endpoint (or manually build it).

#### Example (manual)

```html
<!-- Replace the href with the URL of the track/playlist you want -->
<iframe
    width="100%"               <!-- you can set a fixed width/height if you prefer -->
    height="166"
    scrolling="no"
    frameborder="no"
    allow="autoplay"
    src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&hide_related=false&show_comments=true&show_user=true&show_reposts=false&show_teaser=true&visual=true">
</iframe>
```

*The `src` URL parameters you can tweak:*

| Parameter | Description | Typical values |
|-----------|-------------|----------------|
| `url` | The API URL of the track/playlist (URL‑encoded) | `https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789` |
| `color` | Accent color of the player | Hex code (`%23ff5500` = `#ff5500`) |
| `auto_play` | Start playing automatically | `true` / `false` |
| `visual` | Use the large “visual” player (true) or the classic compact player (false) | `true` / `false` |
| `hide_related`, `show_comments`, `show_user`, `show_reposts`, `show_teaser` | UI toggles | `true` / `false` |

**Result:** A fully functional SoundCloud player appears on your page with no extra JavaScript.

---

## 2️⃣ Custom UI with the SoundCloud **Widget API**

If you want to build your own play/pause button, track list, or progress bar, you still use the same iframe, but you control it via JavaScript.

### 2.1 Register a SoundCloud App (once)

1. Go to **[SoundCloud for Developers → Your Apps](https://developers.soundcloud.com/)**.  
2. Click **“Create a new app”**.  
3. Fill in the name, description, and **Redirect URI** (e.g., `https://yourdomain.com/callback`).  
4. Save – you’ll receive a **Client ID** (and a **Client Secret** if you need OAuth later).

> **Tip:** For just the widget API you only need the **Client ID**. Keep the secret safe; you’ll use it only for server‑side OAuth flows.

### 2.2 Add the Widget Script

```html
<script src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js"></script>
```

### 2.3 HTML Skeleton

```html
<div id="player-container">
  <!-- The iframe that hosts the SoundCloud player -->
  <iframe
      id="sc-widget"
      width="100%"
      height="166"
      scrolling="no"
      frameborder="no"
      allow="autoplay"
      src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/123456789&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&visual=true">
  </iframe>
</div>

<!-- Your custom controls -->
<button id="playBtn">Play</button>
<button id="pauseBtn">Pause</button>
<input type="range" id="seekBar" min="0" max="100" value="0">
<span id="currentTime">0:00</span> / <span id="duration">0:00</span>
```

### 2.4 JavaScript – Hooking Up the Widget

```html
<script>
  // 1️⃣ Grab the iframe and create a widget instance
  const iframeElement = document.getElementById('sc-widget');
  const widget = SC.Widget(iframeElement);   // SC is a global object from api.js

  // 2️⃣ UI elements
  const playBtn   = document.getElementById('playBtn');
  const pauseBtn  = document.getElementById('pauseBtn');
  const seekBar   = document.getElementById('seekBar');
  const curTimeEl = document.getElementById('currentTime');
  const durEl     = document.getElementById('duration');

  // 3️⃣ Basic controls
  playBtn.addEventListener('click', () => widget.play());
  pauseBtn.addEventListener('click', () => widget.pause());

  // 4️⃣ Update UI while the track plays
  widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.READY, () => {
    // Get total duration (ms) once the player is ready
    widget.getDuration(ms => {
      const seconds = Math.floor(ms / 1000);
      durEl.textContent = `${Math.floor(seconds/60)}:${('0'+seconds%60).slice(-2)}`;
    });
  });

  widget.bind(SC.Widget.Events.PLAY_PROGRESS, data => {
    // data.currentPosition and data.duration are in milliseconds
    const posSec = Math.floor(data.currentPosition / 1000);
    const durSec = Math.floor(data.duration / 1000);
    curTimeEl.textContent = `${Math.floor(posSec/60)}:${('0'+posSec%60).slice(-2)}`;

    // Update seek bar (0‑100 range)
    const percent = (data.currentPosition / data.duration) * 100;
    seekBar.value = percent;
  });

  // 5️⃣ Seek when user drags the range input
  seekBar.addEventListener('input', () => {
    widget.getDuration(duration => {
      const newPos = (seekBar.value / 100) * duration;
      widget.seekTo(newPos);
    });
  });
</script>
```

**Result:** You now have a fully custom UI that talks to the SoundCloud player behind the scenes. The widget handles streaming, DRM, and licensing for you, so you don’t have to host the audio files yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ Full‑Featured Integration (OAuth + REST API)

When you need **user‑specific data** (e.g., “Play the logged‑in user’s liked tracks”, “Create a playlist”, “Upload a track”), you must:

1. **Authenticate the user via OAuth** (granting your app permission to act on their behalf).  
2. **Call the SoundCloud REST API** to fetch tracks, playlists, etc.  
3. **Optionally stream the audio yourself** using the `stream_url` you receive (requires the `*` scope or `*`‑like `*` permission).

Below is a concise roadmap, plus sample code for a typical Node.js + Express backend and a vanilla‑JS front‑end.

### 3.1 Register your app (again, if you haven’t)

Same steps as in **2.1** – you’ll need **Client ID**, **Client Secret**, and a **Redirect URI** that points to a route on your server (e.g., `https://yourdomain.com/auth/callback`).

### 3.2 Server‑side OAuth flow (Node/Express example)

```js
// server.js (Node + Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config(); // .env holds CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET, REDIRECT_URI

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// 1️⃣ Redirect user to SoundCloud for login/consent
app.get('/auth/login', (req, res) => {
  const params = querystring.stringify({
    client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
    redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
    response_type: 'code',
    scope: '*',               // request all permissions (adjust if you need less)
    state: 'someRandomString' // optional, helps prevent CSRF
  });
  res.redirect(`https://soundcloud.com/connect?${params}`);
});

// 2️⃣ SoundCloud redirects back here with ?code=...
app.get('/auth/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code } = req.query;
  if (!code) return res.status(400).send('Missing code');

  // Exchange the code for an access token
  const tokenRes = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
    body: querystring.stringify({
      client_id: process.env.CLIENT_ID,
      client_secret: process.env.CLIENT_SECRET,
      redirect_uri: process.env.REDIRECT_URI,
      grant_type: 'authorization_code',
      code
    })
  });
  const tokenData = await tokenRes.json(); // { access_token, refresh_token, expires_in, ... }

  // Store tokenData in a session / DB for later API calls
  // For demo, we just dump it:
  res.json(tokenData);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server listening on ${PORT}`));
```

**What you get:** An `access_token` that you can use in every subsequent API request as `Authorization: OAuth <access_token>`.

### 3.3 Front‑end: Fetch a user’s liked tracks and play them

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>My SoundCloud Player</title>
  <script src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>My Liked Tracks</h1>
  <ul id="trackList"></ul>

  <!-- The hidden iframe that will be used for playback -->
  <iframe id="sc-widget" style="display:none;"></iframe>

  <script>
    // 1️⃣ Replace with the token you got from your server after OAuth
    const ACCESS_TOKEN = 'YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN';

    // 2️⃣ Helper to call the SoundCloud API
    async function scApi(path) {
      const resp = await fetch(`https://api.soundcloud.com${path}?oauth_token=${ACCESS_TOKEN}`);
      if (!resp.ok) throw new Error('API error: ' + resp.status);
      return resp.json();
    }

    // 3️⃣ Load liked tracks
    async function loadLiked() {
      const data = await scApi('/me/likes'); // returns { collection: [{ track: {...} }, ...] }
      const list = document.getElementById('trackList');
      data.collection.forEach(item => {
        const track = item.track;
        const li = document.createElement('li');
        li.textContent = `${track.title} – ${track.user.username}`;
        li.style.cursor = 'pointer';
        li.onclick = () => playTrack(track.id);
        list.appendChild(li);
      });
    }

    // 4️⃣ Play a track using the widget API (creates a new iframe each time)
    function playTrack(trackId) {
      const iframe = document.getElementById('sc-widget');
      // Build the widget URL – note the `auto_play=true` to start immediately
      const src = `https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=${encodeURIComponent('https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/' + trackId)}&auto_play=true`;
      iframe.src = src;
      iframe.style.display = 'block'; // show the player if you want a UI
    }

    // Kick it off
    loadLiked().catch(console.error);
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Key points:**

| Action | API endpoint | What you receive |
|--------|--------------|------------------|
| Get current user profile | `GET /me` | `id`, `username`, `avatar_url`, etc. |
| Get liked tracks | `GET /me/likes` | `collection` array of `{ track: {...} }` |
| Get a user’s playlists | `GET /me/playlists` | playlist objects with `track_count`, `tracks` |
| Upload a new track | `POST /tracks` (multipart/form‑data) | New track object (requires `publishable` scope) |
| Stream a track directly (HTML5 `<audio>`) | `GET /tracks/:id/stream` (requires OAuth token) | A URL you can set as `src` on `<audio>` |

> **Important:** Direct streaming (`/tracks/:id/stream`) works **only** for tracks that the authenticated user has permission to play (public tracks, or private tracks the user owns). For most public playback scenarios, the widget approach is simpler and automatically respects licensing.

### 3.4 Optional: Use the raw `<audio>` element (advanced)

If you really need a completely custom player (e.g., you want to hide the SoundCloud branding entirely), you can fetch the stream URL and feed it to an `<audio>` tag. **Only do this if you have the appropriate commercial license**; otherwise the widget is the recommended, fully‑compliant method.

```html
<audio id="myAudio" controls></audio>

<script>
  async function playDirect(trackId) {
    const resp = await fetch(`https://api.soundcloud.com/tracks/${trackId}/stream?oauth_token=${ACCESS_TOKEN}`);
    const streamUrl = resp.url; // final redirected URL
    const audio = document.getElementById('myAudio');
    audio.src = streamUrl;
    audio.play();
  }
</script>
```

---

## 4️⃣ Styling & Responsiveness

- **Responsive iframe:** Wrap the iframe in a container with `position: relative; padding-bottom: 166px; height: 0;` and set the iframe to `position: absolute; top:0; left:0; width:100%; height:100%;`. This makes the player keep its aspect ratio on mobile.
- **Custom colors:** Use the `color` query‑parameter (hex, URL‑encoded) to match your site’s theme.
- **Hide the default UI:** If you only need audio (no visual player), set `visual=false` and `hide_related=true`. You can then overlay your own controls.

```css
/* Example responsive wrapper */
.player-wrapper {
  position: relative;
  padding-top: 56.25%; /* 16:9 ratio – adjust if you use the compact player */
}
.player-wrapper iframe {
  position: absolute;
  top:0; left:0;
  width:100%; height:100%;
  border:none;
}
```

---

## 5️⃣ Checklist Before Going Live

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **Client ID registered** | Required for widget API and any API calls. |
| **OAuth flow tested** | Users must be able to log in and grant permissions. |
| **CORS & HTTPS** | SoundCloud only serves the widget over HTTPS; your site should also be HTTPS to avoid mixed‑content warnings. |
| **Rate‑limit handling** | SoundCloud limits API calls (≈15,000 per hour per client). Cache results where possible. |
| **License compliance** | If you stream audio yourself (HTML5 `<audio>`), ensure you have the right to do so (commercial use may need a separate agreement). |
| **Responsive design** | Test on mobile, tablets, and desktop. |
| **Accessibility** | Add `title`/`aria-label` to custom buttons, and ensure the `<audio>` element has fallback text. |

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR – Minimal Code to Drop a Player

```html
<!-- 1️⃣ Include the widget script (optional if you only embed) -->
<script src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/api.js"></script>

<!-- 2️⃣ Embed the iframe (replace TRACK_ID) -->
<iframe
    id="sc-widget"
    width="100%" height="166"
    scrolling="no" frameborder="no"
    allow="autoplay"
    src="https://w.soundcloud.com/player/?url=https%3A//api.soundcloud.com/tracks/TRACK_ID&color=%23ff5500&auto_play=false&visual=true">
</iframe>

<!-- 3️⃣ (Optional) Custom controls -->
<button onclick="SC.Widget(document.getElementById('sc-widget')).play()">Play</button>
<button onclick="SC.Widget(document.getElementById('sc-widget')).pause()">Pause</button>
```

That’s all you need for a functional player. From there you can expand to the full Widget API or OAuth‑based integration as shown above.

---

### 🎉 You’re ready!

1. **Start with the embed** to get something on the page instantly.  
2. **Add the Widget API** if you want a custom UI.  
3. **Implement OAuth + REST** when you need user‑specific data or want to upload/manage tracks.

If you run into any specific roadblocks (e.g., “my OAuth callback isn’t working”, “the widget won’t load on Safari”, “I need to display a playlist with thumbnails”), just let me know and I can dive deeper into that particular piece. Happy coding!

### What a “Callback URL” (Redirect URI) Is

When you use **SoundCloud OAuth** to let a user log in and grant your app permissions, the flow works like this:

1. **Your site** sends the user to `https://soundcloud.com/connect?...` with your **client ID**, the **scopes** you need, and a **redirect_uri** (the callback URL).  
2. The user logs in on SoundCloud and clicks **Allow**.  
3. SoundCloud **redirects the browser back** to the URL you supplied, appending a `code` (or an error) as a query‑string parameter.  
4. Your server reads that `code` and exchanges it for an **access token**.

The **callback URL** is simply the endpoint on **your own server** that can receive that redirect and finish the exchange.

---

## 1️⃣ Choosing a Callback URL

| Situation | Recommended URL | Why |
|-----------|----------------|-----|
| **Production website** (e.g., `https://my‑music‑site.com`) | `https://my‑music‑site.com/auth/soundcloud/callback` | A dedicated route that only handles the OAuth response. |
| **Local development** (running on your laptop) | `http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback` (or any port you use) | Works while you test; just remember to register the exact URL in the SoundCloud app settings. |
| **Single‑page app (SPA) without a backend** | `https://my‑app.com/auth/callback.html` (a static HTML page that reads the `code` from the URL and forwards it to your backend via AJAX) | The page can be a tiny “bridge” that passes the code to your API. |
| **Multiple environments (dev / staging / prod)** | Register **each** URL separately (SoundCloud allows multiple redirect URIs). | Keeps each environment isolated and avoids “redirect_uri_mismatch” errors. |

**Key rules:**

1. **Exact match** – The URL you put in the SoundCloud developer console **must be identical** (including protocol, sub‑domain, path, and trailing slash) to the one you send in the OAuth request.
2. **HTTPS preferred** – Production sites should use `https://`. SoundCloud will still accept `http://` for `localhost` during development, but many browsers block mixed‑content if your main site is HTTPS.
3. **No query‑string** (unless you need it) – Keep it simple: `https://example.com/auth/callback`. If you add a query string, you must include it in the registration exactly.

---

## 2️⃣ How to Register It in the SoundCloud Developer Console

1. Go to **[SoundCloud Developers → Your Apps](https://developers.soundcloud.com/)**.  
2. Click the app you created (the one that already has your client ID/secret).  
3. Find the **“Redirect URIs”** (or “Callback URL”) field.  
4. **Enter each URL** you plan to use, one per line, e.g.:

   ```
   https://my-music-site.com/auth/soundcloud/callback
   http://localhost:3000/auth/soundcloud/callback
   ```

5. Save the changes.

If you later add a new environment, just edit the list and save again.

---

## 3️⃣ Minimal Server‑Side Example (Node / Express)

Below is a tiny Express route that can serve as your callback endpoint. Adjust the path to match whatever you registered.

```js
// server.js (Node + Express)
const express = require('express');
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
const querystring = require('querystring');
require('dotenv').config();   // .env holds CLIENT_ID, CLIENT_SECRET, REDIRECT_URI

const app = express();
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

// 1️⃣ This is the **callback URL** you registered with SoundCloud
app.get('/auth/soundcloud/callback', async (req, res) => {
  const { code, error } = req.query;

  if (error) {
    return res.status(400).send(`SoundCloud error: ${error}`);
  }
  if (!code) {
    return res.status(400).send('Missing authorization code');
  }

  // 2️⃣ Exchange the code for an access token
  try {
    const tokenRes = await fetch('https://api.soundcloud.com/oauth2/token', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded' },
